<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Analects</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Analects of Confucius, Lunyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Analects (Lunyu) is a collection of sayings and short dialogues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and his disciples, compiled by later followers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and fixed in its present form in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Its twenty books set out the ideals of humaneness, ritual propriety, filial piety and rule by moral example. It became the central text of Confucian education across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/analects.jpg" alt="Analects" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Confucius and his disciples, · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rongo_Analects_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
